== 1. The Greatness of Sin

#align(center)[An Appendix concerning five more separate #emph[devices] that Satan has to keep poor souls from believing in Christ, from receiving Christ, from embracing Christ, from resting, leaning, or relying on Christ for everlasting happiness and blessedness, according to the gospel; and remedies against those #emph[devices.]]

=== Now the first #emph[device] that Satan has to keep the soul back from believing in Christ, from embracing Christ, etc. is,

BY suggesting to the soul the greatness and vileness of his sins. “What,” says Satan, “do you think that you will ever obtain mercy through Christ, you who have sinned so high-handedly against Christ? You who have slighted the offers of grace? You who have grieved the Spirit of grace? You who have despised the word of grace? You who have trampled underfoot the blood of the covenant, by which you might have been pardoned, purged, justified, and saved? You who have spoken and done all the evil that you could? No, no,” says Satan, “he has mercy for others,#footnote[Jer 3:5.] but not for you; pardon for others, but not for you; righteousness for others, but not for you, etc. Therefore it is vain for you to think of believing in Christ, or of resting and leaning your guilty soul on Christ.”

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider that the greater your#super[+] sins are, the more you need a Savior; the greater your burden is, the more you need someone to help you bear it. The deeper the wound is, the more need there is of the surgeon; the more dangerous the disease is, the more need there is of the physician. Who but madmen will argue like this: “My burden is great, therefore I will not call out for help; my wound is#footnote[Mad logic.] deep, therefore I will not call out for balm; my disease is dangerous, therefore I will not go to the physician”? Ah! It is spiritual madness; it is the devil’s logic to argue like this: “My sins are great, therefore I will not go to Christ; I dare not rest or lean on Christ,” etc. But the soul should reason like this: “The greater my sins are, the more I need mercy, pardon; and therefore I will go to Christ, who delights#footnote[Mic 7:18.] in mercy, who pardons sin for his own name’s sake, who is as able and as#footnote[Isa 43:25.] willing to forgive pounds as pence, thousands as hundreds.”

The second #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is solemnly to consider that the promise of grace and mercy is to returning souls. And therefore, however wicked you are, yet if you will return, God will be yours, mercy will be yours, and pardon yours, etc. 2 Chr 30:9: “For if you#super[+] return to the #smallcaps[Lord],#footnote[2 Chr 30:9.] your brothers and sons will receive mercy in the presence of their captors and will return to this land. For the #smallcaps[Lord] your#super[+] God is gracious and merciful; He will not turn His face away from you if you return to Him.” So Jer 3:12:#footnote[Jer 3:12.] “Go, proclaim this message toward the north: ‘Return, O faithless Israel,’ declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. ‘I will no longer look on you#super[+] with anger, for I am merciful,’ declares the #smallcaps[Lord]. ‘I will not be angry forever.’” So Joel 2:13: “Rend your#super[+]#footnote[Joel 2:13.] hearts and not your garments, and return to the #smallcaps[Lord] your God. For He is gracious and compassionate, slow to anger, abounding in loving devotion. And He relents from sending disaster.” So Isa 55:7: “Let the wicked man forsake his own way and the unrighteous man his own thoughts; let him return to the #smallcaps[Lord], and He will have mercy on him, and to our God, for He will abundantly pardon”; or, as the Hebrew reads it, #emph[he will multiply pardons.] So Ezek#footnote[Ezek 18.] 18.

Ah sinner! It is not your great transgressions that will shut you out from mercy, if you will break off your sins by repentance and return to the fountain of mercy. Christ’s heart, Christ’s arms, are wide open to embrace the returning prodigal. It is not simply the greatest of your sins, but your obstinate persisting in sin, that will be your eternal overthrow.

The third #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is seriously to consider that the greatest sinners have obtained mercy; and therefore all the angels in heaven, all the men on earth, and all the devils in hell cannot say that you may not obtain mercy. Manasseh was a notorious sinner.#footnote[2 Kgs 21.] He set up altars for Baal; he worshiped and served all the host of heaven. #emph[He made his sons pass through the fire; he gave himself to witchcraft and sorcery; he made Judah sin more wickedly than the heathen did, whom the Lord destroyed before the children of Israel; he]#footnote[The Hebrew teachers write that he killed Isaiah the prophet, who was his father-in-law.] #emph[made the streets of Jerusalem run down with innocent blood, etc.] Ah! What a devil incarnate he was in his actions! And yet when he humbled himself and sought the Lord, the Lord listened to his plea, and heard his supplication, and brought him back to Jerusalem, and made himself known to him, and crowned him with mercy and loving-kindness, as you may see in 2 Chr#footnote[2 Chr 33.] 33. So Paul was once “a blasphemer, a persecutor, and a violent man,” yet he#footnote[1 Tim 1:13.] “was shown mercy.” So Mary Magdalene was a#footnote[Luke 7.] notorious prostitute, a common whore, one out of whom Christ cast seven devils; yet she is pardoned by Christ and dearly loved by Christ (Luke 7). So#footnote[Mark 16:9.] in Mark 16:9: “Early on the first day of the week, after Jesus had risen, He appeared first to Mary Magdalene, from whom He had driven out seven demons.” Jansenius, on the passage, says that it is very noteworthy that our Savior after his resurrection appeared first to Mary Magdalene and to Peter, who had been grievous sinners, so that even the worst of sinners may be comforted and encouraged to come to Christ, to believe in Christ, to rest and stay their souls on Christ for mercy here and glory hereafter. A very precious word for the worst of sinners to hang on is Ps 68:18. The#footnote[Ps 68:18.] Psalmist, speaking of Christ, says, “You have ascended on high; You have led captives away. You have received gifts for men, even for the rebellious, that the #smallcaps[Lord] God may dwell among them.”

What if you are a rebellious child or a rebellious servant? What if you are a rebellious swearer, a rebellious drunkard, a rebellious Sabbath-breaker, etc.? Yet #emph[Christ has received gifts for you, even for the rebellious.] He has received the gift of pardon, the gift of righteousness, indeed all the gifts of the Spirit, for you, so that your heart may be made a delightful house for God to dwell in.

Bodin has a story about a great rebel who had raised a great, strong party against a Roman emperor. The emperor makes a proclamation that whoever could bring in the rebel, dead or alive, would have a great sum of money. The rebel, hearing of this, comes and presents himself before the emperor and demands the sum of money. “Now,” says the emperor, “if I put him to death, the world will say I did it to save my money.” And so he pardons the rebel and gives him the money. Ah sinners! Shall a heathen do this, who had only a drop of mercy and compassion in him? And will not Christ do much more,#footnote[Col 1:19; 2:3, 4.] who has all fullness of grace, mercy, and glory in himself? Surely his heart yearns toward the worst of rebels. Ah! If you#super[+] will only come in, you will find him ready to pardon, indeed one#footnote[#emph[Neh 9:17. Hebrew:] “But you are a God of pardons.”] made up of pardoning mercy. Oh, the readiness and willingness of Jesus Christ to receive the greatest rebels into favor!

The father of mercies met, embraced, and kissed that prodigal mouth which came from feeding with swine and kissing harlots. Ephraim had#footnote[See Hos 4:17; 5:3; 6:8; 11:12; 12:14; 13:12.] committed idolatry and had backslidden from God; he was guilty of lukewarmness and unbelief, etc. Yet God says, “Ephraim is My dear son, a delightful#footnote[Jer 31:20.] child. My heart yearns for him; I will have mercy” (or rather, as it is in the original, #emph[I will have mercy, mercy]) “on him, declares the #smallcaps[Lord].” “Well,” says God, #emph[“Though Ephraim is guilty of crimson sins, yet he is a son, a dear son, a precious son, a delightful child. Though he is black with filth and red with guilt, yet my heart yearns for him; I will have mercy, mercy on him.”] Ah sinners! If this tender heart of mercy does not melt, win, and draw you#super[+], justice will be a swift witness against you and will make you lie down in eternal misery for kicking against such tender mercy.

Christ continues to hang out, as that warlike Scythian once did, a white flag of grace and mercy to returning sinners who humble themselves at his feet for favor. But if sinners hold out, Christ will put out his red flag, his bloody flag, and then they will die forever by a hand of justice. Sinners, there is no way to avoid perishing by Christ’s iron rod but by kissing his golden scepter.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider that Jesus Christ has nowhere in all the Scripture excluded the worst of sinners who are willing to receive him, to believe in him, and to rest on him for happiness and blessedness. Ah sinners! Why should you#super[+] be more cruel and unmerciful to your own souls than Christ is? Christ has not excluded you#super[+] from mercy; why should you exclude your own souls from mercy? Oh, that you#super[+] would dwell often on that choice Scripture, John 6:37:#footnote[John 6:37.] “Everyone the Father gives Me will come to Me, and the one who comes to Me I will by no means cast out” (or, as the original has it, #emph[I will not cast out]). “Well,” says Christ, “if any man will come, or is coming, to me, let him be more sinful or less, more unworthy or less; let him be ever so guilty, ever so filthy, ever so rebellious, ever so leprous, etc. Yet if he will only come, I will not, not, not cast him off.” The same is set forth in 1 Cor 6:9, 10, 11:#footnote[1 Cor 6:9, 10, 11.] “Do you#super[+] not know that the wicked will not inherit the kingdom of God? Do not be deceived: Neither the sexually immoral, nor idolaters, nor adulterers, nor men who submit to or perform homosexual acts, nor thieves, nor the greedy, nor drunkards, nor verbal abusers, nor swindlers, will inherit the kingdom of God. And that is what some of you#super[+] were. But you#super[+] were washed, you were sanctified, you were justified, in the name of the Lord Jesus and by the Spirit of our God.”

Ah sinners! Do not think that he who has received such notorious sinners to mercy will reject you#super[+]. He is#footnote[Heb 13:8.] “the same yesterday and today and forever.”

Christ was born in an inn, to show that he receives all comers. His garments were divided into four parts, to show that from whatever part of the world we come, we will be received. If we are naked, Christ has robes to clothe us; if we are homeless, Christ has room to lodge us. That is a choice Scripture, Acts 10:34, 35:#footnote[Acts 10:34, 35.] “Then Peter began to speak: ‘I now truly understand that God does not show favoritism, but welcomes those from every nation who fear Him and do what is right.’”

The three languages that were written on the cross, Greek, Latin, and Hebrew, to witness that Christ was#footnote[John 19:19, 20.] the King of the Jews, each in its own idiom affirms this singular axiom: that Christ is an all-sufficient Savior; and a threefold cord is not easily broken. The apostle puts this beyond doubt in Heb 7:25:#footnote[Heb 7:25.] “Therefore He is able to save to the uttermost those who draw near to God through Him, since He always lives to intercede for them.” Now he would not be an all-sufficient Savior if he were not as able to save the greatest sinners as the least. Ah sinners, tell Jesus Christ that he has not excluded you#super[+] from mercy, and that therefore you are resolved to sit, wait, weep, and knock at the door of mercy until he says, “Souls, take heart! Your#super[+] sins are forgiven, your persons are justified, and your souls will be saved.”

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is to consider that the greater a sinner you are, the dearer you will be to Christ when he sees you as the fruit of the anguish of his soul. Isa 53:11:#footnote[Isa 53:11.] “He will see the fruit of the anguish of His soul and be satisfied.” The more dearly we pay for anything, the dearer that thing is to us. Christ has paid most, and prayed most, and sighed most, and wept most, and bled most for the greatest sinners; and therefore they are dearer to Christ than others who are less sinful. Rachel was#footnote[Gen 29:30.] dearer to Jacob than Leah, because she cost him more; he obeyed, endured, and suffered more by day and by night for her than for Leah. Ah sinners! The greatness of your#super[+] sins only sets off the freeness and riches of Christ’s grace, and the freeness of his love. This makes heaven and earth ring with his praise: that he loves those who are most unlovely, that he shows most favor to those who have sinned most highly against him. This might be shown by various instances in Scripture, such as Paul, Mary Magdalene, and others. Who sinned more against Christ than these? And who had sweeter and choicer manifestations of divine love and favor than these?

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is seriously to consider that the longer you#super[+] keep away from Christ, the greater and stronger your sins will grow. All divine power and strength against sin flows from the soul’s union#footnote[Rom 8:10. 1 John 1:6, 7.] and communion with Christ. While you#super[+] keep away from Christ, you keep away from that strength and power which alone is able to make you trample down strength, lead captivity captive, and slay the Goliaths that defy Christ. It is only faith in Christ that makes a#footnote[1 John 5:4.] man triumph over sin, Satan, hell, and the world. It is only faith in Christ#footnote[Mark 5:25–35.] that binds the strong man hand and foot, that stops the flow of blood, that makes a man strong in resisting and happy in conquering. Sin always dies most where faith lives most; the most believing soul is the most mortified soul. Ah sinner! Remember this: there is no way on earth to be effectually rid of the guilt, filth, and power of sin but by believing in a Savior. It is not resolving, it is not complaining, it is not mourning, but believing, that will make you divinely victorious over that body of sin which to this day is too strong for you, and which will certainly be your ruin if it is not ruined by a hand of faith.

The seventh #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is wisely to consider that, as there is nothing in Christ to discourage the greatest sinners from believing in him, so there is everything in Christ that may encourage the greatest sinners to believe in him, to rest and lean on him for all happiness and blessedness. If you#super[+] look at his nature, his disposition, his names, his titles, his offices as King, priest, and#footnote[Song 1:3.] prophet, you will find nothing to discourage the greatest sinners from receiving#footnote[Col 1:19; 2:3. Song 5:10.] him, from believing on him. Christ is the greatest good, the choicest good, the chief good, the most suitable good, the most necessary good. He is a pure good, a real good, a total good, an eternal good, and a soul-satisfying good. Sinners, are you#super[+] poor? Christ#footnote[Rev 3:17, 18.] has gold to enrich you#super[+]. Are you#super[+] naked? Christ has royal robes; he has white garments to clothe you#super[+]. Are you#super[+] blind? Christ has eye salve to enlighten you#super[+]. Are you#super[+] hungry? Christ will be manna to feed you#super[+]. Are you#super[+] thirsty?#footnote[John 6:48. John 7:38.] He will be a well of living water to refresh you#super[+]. Are you#super[+] wounded? He has balm under his wings to heal you#super[+]. Are#footnote[Mal 4:2. Matt 4:23. Matt 20:28.] you#super[+] sick? He is a physician to cure you#super[+]. Are you#super[+] prisoners? He has laid down a ransom for you#super[+]. Ah sinners! Tell me, tell me, is there anything in Christ to keep you#super[+] from believing? No. Is there not everything in Christ that may encourage you#super[+] to believe in him? Yes. Oh, then believe in him, and then “though your#super[+] sins are like scarlet, they will be as#footnote[Isa 1:18.] white as snow; though they are as red as crimson, they will become like wool.” More than that, your#super[+] iniquities will then be forgotten as well as#footnote[Isa 43:25. Isa 38:17. Mic 7:19.] forgiven; they will be remembered no more. God will cast them behind his back; he will hurl them into the bottom of the sea.

The eighth and last #emph[remedy] against this #emph[device] of Satan is seriously to consider the absolute necessity of believing in Christ. Heaven is too holy and too hot to hold unbelievers; their lodging is prepared in hell. Rev 21:8:#footnote[Rev 21:8.] “But to the cowardly and unbelieving,” etc., “their place will be in the lake that burns with fire and sulfur. This is the second death.” “Unless you#super[+] believe that I am He,” says Christ,#footnote[John 8:24.] “you will die in your sins.” And he who dies in his sins must go to judgment, and to hell, in his sins. Every unbeliever is a condemned man: “Whoever does not believe,” says John, “has already been condemned, because#footnote[John 3:18.] he has not believed in the name of God’s one and only Son.” And “whoever does not#footnote[Verse 36.] believe the Son will not see life. Instead, the wrath of God remains on him.” Ah sinners! The law, the gospel, and your#super[+] own consciences have passed the sentence of condemnation on you, and there is no way to reverse the sentence but by believing in Christ. And therefore my counsel is this: stir#footnote[Isa 64:7.] yourselves up to lay hold on the Lord Jesus, and look up to him, and wait on him from whom every good and perfect#footnote[Jas 1:17. Isa 62:7.] gift comes, and give him no rest until he has given you that jewel, faith, which is worth more than heaven and earth, and which will make you happy in life, joyful in death, and glorious in the day of Christ. And so much for the #emph[remedies] against this first #emph[device] of Satan, by which he keeps thousands from believing in Christ.
